PASSWORD RULE CHECK

Password Rule Check — a real-world task using only today's concepts: checking with in, for loops, len(), comparisons, if / elif / else, counting with variables, variables, input(), and printing. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A fintech onboarding screen checks how strong a new password is BEFORE accepting it. The checker reports four rules, then gives a verdict.

The four rules:
1. Length: at least 8 characters.
2. Special character: contains @ or # somewhere in it.
3. Digit: contains at least one character from 0123456789 (walk through the password one character at a time with a for loop and check each character with in).
4. No spaces: a space anywhere fails this rule.

The program asks for the password once and prints exactly five lines:

LENGTH: OK
SPECIAL: FOUND
DIGIT: MISSING
SPACES: NONE FOUND
VERDICT: WEAK - fix the missing rules

STRONG is printed in the verdict only when all four rules pass. Anything else is WEAK.

Sample run:

Password: ada@2026x

LENGTH: OK
SPECIAL: FOUND
DIGIT: FOUND
SPACES: NONE FOUND
VERDICT: STRONG - all rules passed

Edge cases to handle: exactly 8 characters (OK), exactly 7 (TOO SHORT), a password with a space in the middle, a password with @ but no digit, a password with a digit but no @ or #, and the empty password (fails everything without crashing).
